{"archetypes":[{"name":"find-portability","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"The core of the refactor replaces epoch-math with find -mtime +N; BSD find (macOS) and GNU find differ in truncation semantics and the +N means 'strictly more than N 24-hour periods ago', which may not be identical to the removed NOW-CUTOFF arithmetic on boundary days.","prompt_body":"Verify that `find -mtime +\"$RETENTION_DAYS\"` is semantically equivalent to the removed `newest < NOW - RETENTION_DAYS * 86400` guard on both macOS BSD find and GNU/Linux find, paying attention to the truncation difference ('rounding to integer number of 24h periods' vs exact epoch subtraction). Check whether passing an integer from a variable (`+\"$RETENTION_DAYS\"`) is valid syntax for both find implementations and whether the OR-clause construction in `_find_name_args` — building `-name \"p1\" -o -name \"p2\" ...` then wrapping in `\\( ... \\)` — is correctly grouped and portable. Confirm the combined `-mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 ! -type l -mtime +N \\( name-clause \\)` predicate is evaluated with the intended precedence on both platforms. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"deletion-guard-regression","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"The old code had an explicit [[ -d && ! -L ]] guard plus a per-entry existence check before rm -rf; the new code removes both and relies entirely on find ! -type l, creating a potential TOCTOU window and different behavior for plain files vs directories in the /tmp pass.","prompt_body":"Examine whether the removal of `[[ -d \"$entry\" && ! -L \"$entry\" ]]` and the `[[ -e \"$entry\" || -L \"$entry\" ]]` existence guard in `cleanup.sh` is safe given that `find ! -type l` snapshots entries at enumeration time and `rm -rf` runs later. Pay particular attention to the `/tmp` pass where the old code explicitly branched on `-d` (depth-5 removal) vs `-f` (stat-only file removal) and now `rm -rf` is applied uniformly to both; verify `rm -rf` on a plain file is correct. Check whether the `|| true` on the `while ... done < <(find ... -print0 2>/dev/null)` construct is sufficient to prevent `set -euo pipefail` from aborting when `find` itself errors out or returns non-zero on some platforms. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"test-fixture-mtime","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"The new stale-toplevel-with-fresh-deep-child-removed case depends on touch -t STALE_TS correctly overriding the parent dir mtime AFTER writing the child; if the write order or touch-t syntax is wrong the case silently tests the wrong thing.","prompt_body":"Review `test-cleanup.sh`'s mtime fixture setup for `stale-toplevel-with-fresh-deep-child-removed`: confirm that writing `child.txt` and then running `touch -t STALE_TS -- parent` last is sufficient to set the parent directory's mtime to the stale timestamp, accounting for any filesystem buffering or ordering concern. Verify that `touch -t 200001010000` and `touch -t 209901010000` are valid format strings on macOS (`[[CC]YY]MMDDhhmm[.ss]`) and that `find -mtime +7` will reliably classify the year-2000 fixture as stale and the year-2099 fixture as fresh at any point before 2099. Check whether the `SECONDS=0` timing guard in `large-tmp-scales` is reliable in CI (bash built-in reset semantics, sub-second resolution), and whether 2000 non-matching `mkdir` calls within a test harness create a meaningful timing risk or false positives. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
